    "result_text": "All green. The full suite (`AcceptanceTest`) passes and the build succeeds, which confirms the generated `OwnerDto` now compiles with `membershipLevel` (and no `membershipTier`).\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaced the string `membershipTier` with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133), assigned at creation.\n\n**The rule, in one place** \u2014 `OwnerMapper.membershipLevel(Owner)` (`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`): starts at 1, +1 when a non-blank email is present, +1 when `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3 via `Math.min` (leaving room for the tenure-reserved level 4). This lives alongside the other derived owner attributes (`membershipNumber`, `displayName`, `locality`), matching the codebase's convention, and dropped the now-obsolete `householdSize`/GOLD logic.\n\n**Response DTO** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: swapped the `membershipTier` string property for a `membershipLevel` integer (min 1, max 3), so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes the new field and the old one is gone. The mapper's `@Mapping` target follows.\n\n**Audit line** \u2014 `AuditOwnerCreated`: now records `membershipLevel=` on the create audit line, reusing `ownerMapper.membershipLevel(owner)` rather than re-deriving it (the mapper is a Spring bean, injected the same way `RespondWithOwnerCreated` already does).\n\nThe new `coreNumericLevel` test \u2014 a unique owner (namesakeCount 0) with an email \u2014 asserts `membershipLevel` of 3 (1 + 1 + 1, capped), which passes; the audit log confirms `membershipLevel=3`.",
